>
  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Independent retail-shareholder project</p>
      <h1>Unvoted shares can be the difference.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        On October 22, 2026, both companies told the SEC that special committees are exploring a
        deeper relationship, including a possible combination. There are no terms and no proxy.
        This site explains what is on the record, how a TSLA vote is likely to be counted, and how
        to cast your own when there is a ballot.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button" href="/the-vote/">Read the vote explainer</a>
        <a class="button secondary" href="/how-to-vote/">How to vote</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="docket" aria-label="What is on the record">
      <article>
        <p class="kicker">TSLA issuer</p>
        <p>
          The board formed a special committee of disinterested directors to explore a deeper
          relationship with SpaceX, which could include a business combination. Nothing is assured.
        </p>
        <p class="source">
          <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1318605/000162828026066775/tsla-20260930.htm">Form 10-Q</a>
          · filed October 22, 2026 · quarter ended September 30, 2026
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="kicker">SpaceX</p>
        <p>
          Its board formed a special committee to evaluate a potential combination. Any deal would
          also need its board and its controlling shareholder. No determination has been made.
        </p>
        <p class="source">
          <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1181412/000162828026066880/spcx-20260930.htm">Form 10-Q</a>
          · filed October 22, 2026 · quarter ended September 30, 2026
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="kicker">Not filed</p>
        <p>
          No agreement, price, exchange ratio, or proxy for a combination is in those reports.
          A proxy, if one is filed later, is the document that would set the voting standard.
        </p>
        <p class="source">As of October 24, 2026 · see <a href="/the-vote/">The vote</a></p>
      </article>
    </section>

    <div class="split">
      <section class="prose">
        <h2>Why turnout matters</h2>
        <p>
          Texas law’s default for a merger is two-thirds of the outstanding shares entitled to vote.
          The Texas certificate of formation filed when the TSLA issuer converted in 2024 uses
          different wording: a majority of the shares entitled to vote and then outstanding, including
          for a “fundamental business transaction.” That sentence is subject to other parts of the
          certificate and the bylaws.
        </p>
        <p>
          If a future proxy uses a majority-of-outstanding standard, only yes votes fill the
          numerator. Shares that abstain, that a broker cannot vote, or that never come back do not
          add a yes. They make the threshold harder to reach. That reading is
          <strong>unconfirmed</strong> until a proxy says what standard applies to the actual transaction.
        </p>
        <p>
          There is a recent illustration that is confirmed. At the November 6, 2025 annual meeting,
          a shareholder proposal on an xAI investment received more votes for than against and still
          failed, because the company’s bylaws generally count abstentions as votes against.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/the-vote/">The full explanation, with the filing quotes and the vote counts.</a></p>
      </section>
      <aside class="callout">
        <p class="kicker">A different meeting is already scheduled</p>
        <p>
          <strong>December 3, 2026 annual meeting.</strong>
          A definitive proxy for that meeting was filed on October 19, 2026. It is not a combination
          vote. The voluntary retail voting program does not cast a ballot at that meeting.
        </p>
        <p class="source">
          <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1318605/000110465926116716/tm267596d6_defa14a.htm">DEFA14A filed October 19, 2026</a>
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>

